+#!/bin/sh
+set -eu
+cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
+: "${SECRET_KEY_BASE:?SECRET_KEY_BASE must be set}"
+: "${QUIZZY_MODERATOR_USERNAME:?QUIZZY_MODERATOR_USERNAME must be set}"
+: "${QUIZZY_MODERATOR_PASSWORD:?QUIZZY_MODERATOR_PASSWORD must be set}"
+mkdir -p storage
+cargo build --release --offline --locked
+exec ./target/release/quizzy
diff --git a/docs/implementation.md b/docs/implementation.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5af9acd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/implementation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,215 @@
+# Quizzy implementation notes
+
+- Start with `bin/serve`. Required environment: `SECRET_KEY_BASE`,
+  `QUIZZY_MODERATOR_USERNAME`, `QUIZZY_MODERATOR_PASSWORD`; `PORT` defaults to 3000.
+  The launcher builds offline in release mode, then execs the foreground server.
+- SQLite schema is initialized on startup; all runtime data lives in
+  `storage/quizzy.sqlite3` (including SQLite journal files). Foreign keys cascade
+  deletes; question/options saves and question reordering are transactional.
+- Every `/moderate` route, including preview and unknown nested paths, requires
+  HTTP Basic authentication. Mutations are POST forms protected with a token
+  derived from SECRET_KEY_BASE and the moderator credentials. Plain HTTP and
+  arbitrary hosts are intentional deployment requirements.
+- Assets are local, embedded in the binary. The supplied Inter font and stylesheet
+  are used directly, with a small companion stylesheet for responsive layouts.
+- Preview is a snapshot of active questions in order. Answers and score live only
+  in page memory: no cookies, local/session storage, database writes or API calls.
+  Refreshing or Start over resets it. Multiple correct choices are accepted, but
+  each preview question only permits a single selection and awards at most one point.
+- Option inputs use numbered names and are parsed in numeric order. Empty text is
+  excluded before checking the two-option and correct-option rules. Failed forms
+  retain the original input, including blank rows and their checkbox values.
+
+## Verification and design checks
+
+- `tests/smoke.py`: HTTP Basic coverage (including unknown paths and both root
+  slash spellings), CSRF, validation, retained values, escaping, reorder boundaries,
+  activation, parent-quiz isolation, preview filtering, and cascaded deletion.
+- `tests/browser.py`: real Chromium clicks and native dialogs, creation/editing,
+  added/removed option rows, invalid submissions, ordering, persistence of toggles,
+  empty/single/multiple-question previews, multi-correct feedback, scoring, reset,
+  and mobile overflow checks. It asserts that preview interactions send no requests
+  and create no cookies or web storage entries.
+- `tests/persistence.py`: starts the actual offline launcher, restarts it, and
+  verifies title, question order, options, correct/active flags, and deletion.
+- Mockup and app desktop checks use 1440×1080; phone checks use 390×844. Both use
+  Inter, the original lavender #e9e6ff, and the stylesheet's 72px desktop h1.
+  The source design CSS and font are unchanged in assets/; app.css adds responsive
+  stacking, semantic-list resets, and keyboard focus styling.
+
+## Quiz content transfer
+
+- `GET /moderate/quizzes/{id}/export` renders the only `<pre>` on the page.
+  `GET /moderate/quizzes/import` renders the import form; POST to that same path
+  parses `quiz_json` and redirects to the newly created quiz. Both are covered by
+  moderator authentication; POST also uses the existing CSRF token.
+- `transfer::QuizContents` is the portable DTO: only `title` and `questions`.
+  Questions use `QuestionInput` (`text`, `active`, `options`), and options use
+  `Choice` (`text`, `correct`). No database IDs or preview/runtime state can be
+  serialized through it. Database reads explicitly order questions and options.
+- Required JSON fields must have the stated types. Only omitted `active` and
+  `correct` fields get defaults (`true` and `false` respectively); `null`, strings,
+  and numbers are not coerced into booleans. Explicit object-shape checks prevent
+  Serde from accepting positional arrays as structs. Unknown fields are ignored
+  on import and never exported. Empty quizzes are allowed; blank titles are not.
+- Validation calls the same `QuestionInput::errors()` as the question form, and
+  both paths share database insertion and `saved_options()` normalization. Text
+  is trimmed, blank option rows are skipped, and at least two remaining options
+  and one remaining correct choice are required. Errors identify question numbers.
+- The entire JSON document is parsed and validated before any database write.
+  The quiz and every question/option are then inserted in one SQLite transaction;
+  database failure rolls everything back. Titles are not deduplicated. There is
+  no schema change for this feature.
+- Export JSON and retained textarea contents are HTML-escaped, so embedded
+  `</pre>`, `</textarea>`, script tags, ampersands, and Unicode stay copyable text.
+- Tests: `cargo test --offline` includes a trigger-induced failure on the last
+  option to verify transaction rollback, exact output keys/order/defaults, and
+  malformed schemas. `tests/import_export.py` covers HTTP transfers and atomic
+  validation failures. `tests/import_export_browser.py` uses actual Chromium
+  navigation and text insertion to verify copying from the `<pre>`, pasting into
+  the labeled textarea, importing twice, error retention, and mobile layout.
+  `tests/persistence.py` also verifies imported contents across process restarts.
+
+## Live games
+
+- `POST /moderate/quizzes/:id/games` creates a new random-code game and redirects
+  to `/moderate/games/:code`. The existing HTTP Basic boundary protects the page,
+  JSON state, SSE, actions, and QR image. Quiz pages list previous games, each in
+  its own list item. Starting a game requires the existing moderator CSRF token.
+- Games snapshot the title and **ordered active questions** in a transaction.
+  Template edits/reordering/activation do not change an existing game. A new game
+  gets new players and answers. Deleting the parent quiz cascades to its games,
+  players, and answers. Export remains template-only.
+- State machine: `waiting → open → closed → open … → closed → ended`. Start on an
+  empty snapshot ends immediately. A version-checked moderator action prevents
+  double clicks, retries, and stale tabs from skipping questions. Answers and
+  transitions are serialized by the SQLite connection mutex; answer upsert and
+  revision increment commit together. Late/stale/out-of-range submissions cannot
+  alter a closed question or answer a different question.
+- The public join URL is `http://<request Host>/play/:code`; it intentionally uses
+  plain HTTP and accepts arbitrary hostnames. Its QR is generated locally as SVG
+  by the `qrcode` crate, using exactly the same URL helper. There is no QR API/CDN.
+- A random 256-bit, game-scoped, persistent HttpOnly/SameSite cookie identifies a
+  player. It is not an account. Reloads, browser restarts, and multiple tabs resume
+  the same identity; different games use different cookies. Duplicate display
+  names are allowed. Names are trimmed, limited to 80 characters, and escaped.
+  Player mutations require a separate HMAC-derived CSRF token; join also checks
+  Origin when supplied. No correctness data, future questions, other players'
+  tokens, or moderator secrets are in player HTML/JSON/SSE before Finish. Explicit
+  projections, not serialization of stored questions, enforce this boundary.
+- After Finish, option totals include every saved answer (one final selection per
+  player/question) and all correct choices are marked. Each phone gets its own
+  `Correct!`, `Wrong!`, or `No answer`. A closed game refuses all new joins, and
+  waiting join forms also receive the end state live.
+
+### Conference Wi-Fi and presence
+
+- One same-origin EventSource per page pushes a **full current public snapshot**
+  on connect/reconnect, so no replay log or fragile sequence of deltas is needed.
+  Per-game Tokio watch channels retain only the latest invalidation, wake
+  readers immediately, and never build an unbounded queue behind a slow phone. State is
+  sent only when visible data changed: another player's answer does not cause a
+  full payload to every phone. EventSource has a two-second reconnect delay;
+  application ping events and a 35-second client watchdog recover silent stalls.
+  Online/visibility/page lifecycle handlers support phone sleep and navigation.
+- Players are counted once across their connected tabs. Closing the last stream
+  removes them, without deleting identity or answers. Browser heartbeats renew a
+  45-second presence lease; abandoned TCP connections stop counting after that
+  lease (the stage checks at least every 10 seconds). Ping-event acknowledgements
+  also renew leases in background tabs. Presence is transient and re-established
+  after restart; all gameplay records remain in `storage/quizzy.sqlite3`.
+- The stage shows **saved answers / currently connected players**. A departing
+  player does not erase their saved answer, so the numerator can exceed the
+  denominator when answered players disconnect. Closed-question totals always
+  include those saved answers. There is no public roster or individual answers
+  disclosed to other players.
+- Phone taps are submitted immediately, serialized with a newest-pick queue, and
+  retried after network failures. “Saving your answer…” is distinct from “Answer
+  saved.” An old HTTP response cannot overwrite a newer game revision. Moderator
+  controls are not blindly retried; the client refreshes current state first.
+- All game responses are `no-store`; CSP and same-origin requests apply as on
+  editor pages. SIGINT/SIGTERM stops SSE streams before graceful server shutdown.
+  This is a single-process application; the in-memory hub is not multi-node pubsub.
+
+### Live verification
+
+- `tests/live_game.py`: HTTP lifecycle, CSRF/auth boundaries, hidden correctness and
+  future questions, inactive filtering, changing answers, no-answer/wrong/correct
+  feedback, stale transitions, closed answers, late/end joins, independent games,
+  and the zero-active-question edge case.
+- `tests/live_game_browser.py`: independent Chromium phone/moderator contexts,
+  QR rendering, exact labels, live counters/questions/results without navigation,
+  multiple tabs, remembered identity/answers, leaving/rejoining, late joins,
+  rapid choices, actual network-offline retry, end screens, and narrow layouts.
+- `tests/live_game_persistence.py`: offline `bin/serve`, shutdown with an SSE
+  connection open, immutable game snapshots, and persisted identity/answers and
+  open/closed/ended phases over server restarts.
+- Rust tests additionally exercise public projections, lease expiration,
+  multi-tab presence, and cascading cleanup. Existing CRUD/import/export/preview
+  browser and HTTP suites continue to pass.
+
+
+## Scores and prizes
+
+- `quizzes.number_of_winners` is an integer, default 3, with HTML, server, and
+  SQLite validation (minimum 1). Games snapshot it at creation. Settings edits
+  therefore affect future games, not already-running games. The portable JSON
+  accepts `number_of_winners`, defaults missing values to 3, and emits it when
+  non-default, preserving the original default export shape.
+- Opening a question stores `games.opened_at_us` on the server in the same
+  transaction as its phase/version change. Accepted answer upserts replace both
+  the choice and `answers.elapsed_us`. Time is server receipt time minus opening
+  time, in microseconds, not a client-supplied duration. Clock and answer durations
+  survive restarts; negative wall-clock differences are clamped to zero. The
+  server clock should be kept stable during a conference. Client clocks cannot
+  influence ranking. Delivery cannot be literally simultaneous across a network,
+  but SSE invalidations have no polling/batching delay (local browser tests
+  observed 5–29 ms spread across six screens).
+- Every phone tap has a random `pick_token`; retries retain it. Repeated receipt
+  of the current token does not change its choice or duration. A new tap, even
+  on the same option, has a new token and replaces the duration. Existing clients
+  without a token remain supported and each request counts as a pick. No server
+  correctness, points, ranking, or timing data is exposed to phones during play.
+- `src/prizes.rs` derives points from each game's immutable question snapshot and
+  final answers. Only correct answers contribute a point and elapsed time. The
+  leaderboard includes *all registered players*, not only currently connected
+  phones. Sort order: descending points, ascending sum of correct-answer times,
+  then ascending player ID for a deterministic order on exact time ties. Places
+  are unique, 1-based. Wrong answers and missing answers contribute neither time
+  nor points. No stored/cached score can drift from the answers.
+- Top N filters zero scores and takes the first N. Draw finds the Nth eligible
+  score (or the last eligible score when fewer exist), guarantees players above
+  it, and shuffles the tied pool with unbiased Fisher–Yates using local OS
+  randomness. Speed is irrelevant to the draw. Each draw replaces the previous
+  set; a repeated result is valid. Empty/all-zero games have no winners.
+- Winner flags live on game-scoped players. Authenticated, CSRF-protected,
+  version-checked actions are permitted only after the game ends. Reset, new
+  flags, and version/revision changes commit atomically. There are no client-side
+  decisions or external draw services.
+- Ended SSE streams stay open on both server and browser, and ended pages also
+  subscribe when reloaded. Winner updates use the same full-state projection and
+  revision guard as gameplay. Only the moderator gets the complete leaderboard;
+  a phone receives only its own final place/points/winner flag and player count.
+- Additive, idempotent migrations upgrade existing databases without deleting
+  quizzes or game records. Historic answers made before timing existed receive
+  elapsed time 0 (their true speed cannot be reconstructed). A legacy question
+  still open at upgrade starts its clock once at upgrade; subsequent restarts
+  never reset it. New games have fully measured timing from their first question.
+
+### Prize verification
+
+- `tests/prizes.py`: reproduces the original missing-setting failure; covers
+  validation, persisted/snapshotted prize count, last-pick ranking, points,
+  registered zero-point/missing-answer players, rerolls, top/reset/auth, fewer
+  eligible players than prizes, empty games, and all-zero games.
+- `tests/prizes_browser.py`: actual isolated Chromium contexts for four phones
+  and two moderators; form defaults/validation, measured delivery spread, no
+  points during play, exact labels and separate leaderboard list items, live
+  top/draw/reset from either moderator tab, reconnecting an ended phone, no
+  reloads, and desktop/mobile layout checks/screenshots.
+- `tests/live_game_persistence.py` now verifies stored opening clock, final-answer
+  duration, rankings, winners, reset, and graceful shutdown with an ended stream
+  held open, across offline launcher restarts.
+- Rust unit tests cover total correct-answer time (not fastest/first answer),
+  ignoring wrong-answer time, final-pick timing and idempotent retries, empty and
+  sparse winner pools, reroll tie boundaries and no duplicate/zero-point winners.
